# Project decisions — the *how & why*.
#
# A decision pins an implementation choice over the alternatives that
# were considered. Each decision has:
#   - a stable id (e.g. DEC-001), shared id grammar with requirements
#     and open questions
#   - a status: `accepted` (drives current behavior) or `superseded`
#     (kept as reasoning trail; codegen prompt walks skip these)
#   - a one-line `title` and full `text` body
#   - optional prose: `context` (why we needed to decide),
#     `consequences` (what follows), `alternatives` (what we rejected),
#     `verification` (how we'll know we got it right)
#
# Decisions cross-reference the rest of the spec via:
#   - requirement.decided_by[]   — pin the *how* of a requirement
#   - module.guided_by[]         — inform a module without pinning a req
#   - architecture.project_decisions[] — shape the project as a whole
#   - decisions.toml's own closes_questions[] / defaults_for[] —
#     resolve open questions or commit working defaults
#
# Supersession keeps both bodies. When DEC-N supersedes DEC-M:
#   - DEC-M.status flips to `superseded` and gains
#     `superseded_by = "DEC-N"`
#   - DEC-N (status = accepted) optionally lists `supersedes = ["DEC-M"]`
# The codegen prompt walk follows only `accepted` decisions.
#
# Delete the EXAMPLE entries below once the project has real decisions.

# EXAMPLE: an accepted decision pinning a requirement's implementation.
# [[decision]]
# id = "DEC-001"
# title = "Use `thiserror` for the public error type"
# status = "accepted"
# text = """
# Define the crate's public error type as an enum derived via
# `thiserror::Error`. Variants map 1:1 to the failure modes called
# out in REQ-ERR-001..004; transparent `#[from]` conversions wire
# upstream io / parse errors.
# """
# consequences = """
# `thiserror` is added to runtime deps (not just dev-deps). Public
# enum variants become semver-load-bearing — adding a variant is a
# minor bump unless `#[non_exhaustive]` is set (it is).
# """
# alternatives = """
# Hand-rolled enum + Display impl: rejected as boilerplate-heavy.
# `anyhow::Error` for the public surface: rejected — anyhow is for
# applications, libraries should expose typed errors.
# """
# source = ["rust-api-guidelines"]
# source_ref = "C-GOOD-ERR"

# EXAMPLE: a decision that resolves an open question.
# [[decision]]
# id = "DEC-002"
# title = "Default feature set ships `serde` integration"
# status = "accepted"
# text = """
# The crate's default features include `serde`. Library consumers
# overwhelmingly want the integration; the +1 dep is acceptable.
# """
# closes_questions = ["OQ-001"]

